+# Log Level Filter
+
+CLI tool that filters log lines by severity level. Reads from files or stdin, detects log levels, and outputs only lines matching the minimum severity.
+
+## Features
+
+- Detect log levels: DEBUG, INFO, WARN, ERROR, FATAL
+- Filter by minimum severity level
+- Color-coded output
+- Count mode (match stats only)
+- Read from files, globs, or stdin
+- Single binary, zero dependencies
+
+## Install
+
+```bash
+# From source
+go install github.com/TataneSan/log-level-filter@latest
+
+# Or build locally
+go build -o log-level-filter .
+```
+
+## Usage
+
+```
+log-level-filter [OPTIONS] [FILE...]
+```
+
+### Options
+
+| Flag | Description | Default |
+|------|-------------|---------|
+| `-l, --level LEVEL` | Minimum level (DEBUG, INFO, WARN, ERROR, FATAL) | `INFO` |
+| `-c, --color` | Color output by level | off |
+| `-n, --count` | Only show match count | off |
+| `-h, --help` | Show help | |
+
+### Examples
+
+Filter errors from a log file:
+```bash
+log-level-filter -l ERROR app.log
+```
+
+Color-coded warnings and above:
+```bash
+log-level-filter -l WARN --color access.log
+```
+
+Pipe from another command:
+```bash
+docker logs myapp | log-level-filter -l ERROR
+```
+
+Count matching lines:
+```bash
+log-level-filter -l ERROR --count *.log
+# 42 / 1523 lines (level >= ERROR)
+```
+
+Multiple files with glob:
+```bash
+log-level-filter -l WARN /var/log/app/*.log
+```
+
+## Log Level Detection
+
+The tool recognizes common log level patterns:
+- `[ERROR]`, `ERROR:`, ` error `, `(error)`, etc.
+- Supports: DEBUG, INFO, WARN/WARNING, ERROR/ERR, FATAL/CRITICAL/CRIT
+
+## License
+
+MIT
